CLI-OUT-01 | {'id': 'CLI-OUT-01', 'criterion': 'When stdout is a terminal and NO_COLOR is unset, `status` and `release --dry-run` render a box-drawing table with ANSI colour; when stdout is piped they print plain indented lines with no ANSI codes or box-drawing characters.'}
CLI-OUT-02 | {'id': 'CLI-OUT-02', 'criterion': 'A non-empty NO_COLOR disables colour and tables even when CLICOLOR_FORCE or FORCE_COLOR is set; an empty NO_COLOR is ignored.'}
CLI-OUT-03 | {'id': 'CLI-OUT-03', 'criterion': 'CLICOLOR_FORCE or FORCE_COLOR (any value) turns colour and tables on for piped stdout, and CLICOLOR=0 turns them off on a terminal.'}
CLI-OUT-04 | {'id': 'CLI-OUT-04', 'criterion': 'Text output of status, add, init, pre, snapshot and release --dry-run never contains a schema version string such as `schema v1`.'}
CLI-OUT-05 | {'id': 'CLI-OUT-05', 'criterion': 'With `--format json`, the status, add, init, pre and snapshot reports on stdout are single JSON objects with camelCase keys and a top-level `schemaVersion`, `command` (the subcommand name) and `dryRun`. The status, version, snapshot and matrix reports carry `schemaVersion` 2 and name every package `ecosystem/name` for its primary ecosystem, promoted or not; the other reports carry 1.'}
CLI-OUT-06 | {'id': 'CLI-OUT-06', 'criterion': 'When a command fails under `--format json`, stderr ends with a JSON object `{"schemaVersion", "command", "error": {"code", "message", "help", "path"}}` and the exit code is 1; `command` is the subcommand that was parsed and `path` is `null` unless the error carries a structured filesystem path.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-OUT-07 | {'id': 'CLI-OUT-07', 'criterion': 'When a command fails under text format, stderr shows a diagnostic with an error code and a message, and the exit code is 1.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-OUT-08 | {'id': 'CLI-OUT-08', 'criterion': "Argument parsing errors (unknown flag, missing required argument, invalid value) exit 2 with clap's usage message; every other failure exits 1 and every success exits 0, including --dry-run previews and `release` reporting `Nothing to release.`", 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-OUT-09 | {'id': 'CLI-OUT-09', 'criterion': "`completions <shell>` and `schema` exit 0 without printing an error or panicking when stdout's reading end is already closed (e.g. piped into a command that exits early); a `--format json` report command's write error in that same situation propagates as `E213` and exits 1."}
CLI-STATUS-01 | {'id': 'CLI-STATUS-01', 'criterion': '`callisto status`, with or without a callisto.toml, prints one row per package with its name, current version and planned bump (or `none`), then `<N> package(s) pending`, then a `Diagnostics:` section when any exist.'}
CLI-STATUS-02 | {'id': 'CLI-STATUS-02', 'criterion': 'A package with no changeset of its own that `callisto version` would bump through dependency cascade or a fixed or linked group shows that planned bump in status and counts toward the pending total.'}
CLI-STATUS-03 | {'id': 'CLI-STATUS-03', 'criterion': '`status --format json` reports `hasChangesets`, `pending` (count of packages with a planned bump), and per package `package`, `currentVersion`, `lastReleasedVersion`, `pendingSeverity` (omitted when no bump is planned), `changedSinceLastTag`, `releaseTrigger` and `pendingChangesets`. `changedSinceLastTag` is true for a package with no tag, when any directory of the package holds different files than at its last tag, or when its tracked files have uncommitted changes; a change that was later reverted does not count.'}
CLI-STATUS-04 | {'id': 'CLI-STATUS-04', 'criterion': '`callisto status` without --check always exits 0; `status --check` exits 0 when no diagnostic is error-level, regardless of pending changesets, and exits 1 when any diagnostic is error-level.'}
CLI-STATUS-05 | {'id': 'CLI-STATUS-05', 'criterion': 'A changeset naming an unknown, ambiguous or invalid package produces an error-level diagnostic naming the changeset file, so `status --check` exits 1; a changeset with no entries at all is an info-level diagnostic and never fails `status --check`; a changeset that fails to parse (blank summary with entries, malformed entry, missing or unclosed frontmatter) produces an error-level diagnostic naming the file and the parse error, so `status --check` exits 1.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-STATUS-06 | {'id': 'CLI-STATUS-06', 'criterion': '`status --strict` reports strict-escalatable warnings as errors, so `status --check --strict` exits 1 where `status --check` exits 0.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-STATUS-07 | {'id': 'CLI-STATUS-07', 'criterion': '`status` reports an `inferred-release-without-changeset` warning for a package whose pending release is inferred from commits and that no changeset names, because the release pull request reads changesets only; a package a changeset names, or one bumped by a dependency or group, is not reported.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-ADD-01 | {'id': 'CLI-ADD-01', 'criterion': '`callisto add --package <name>:<severity> --summary <text>` (--package repeatable, each name ecosystem-qualified such as `cargo/app`; a name without its ecosystem is E317; severity none, patch, minor or major) writes `.changeset/<adjective>-<noun>-<verb>.md`, choosing a name no file in `.changeset` already has and never replacing one, even if another `callisto add` created it a moment earlier, with front matter mapping each package to its severity and the trimmed summary as body, prints `Added changeset: <path>`, and never prompts.'}
CLI-ADD-02 | {'id': 'CLI-ADD-02', 'criterion': '`add --format json` prints `{"schemaVersion", "command": "add", "dryRun": false, "path"}`; with --dry-run it prints `"dryRun": true`, the path and the file content, and creates no file or directory.'}
CLI-ADD-03 | {'id': 'CLI-ADD-03', 'criterion': '`callisto add` with no --package and a terminal stdin runs a wizard asking, in order, which packages to include, which are major, which remaining are minor (the rest are patch), a non-empty summary unless --summary was given, and a final confirmation; declining the confirmation writes nothing and exits 0. Every wizard prompt and preview line prints to stderr, not stdout.'}
CLI-ADD-04 | {'id': 'CLI-ADD-04', 'criterion': '`callisto add` with no --package and a non-terminal stdin exits 1 with error `E214` whose help names `callisto add --package <name>:<severity>`.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-ADD-05 | {'id': 'CLI-ADD-05', 'criterion': '`add --package` without --summary, or with a whitespace-only summary, exits 1 naming --summary and writes nothing.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-ADD-06 | {'id': 'CLI-ADD-06', 'criterion': 'A --package value missing `:severity`, with an unknown severity, or naming a package not in the workspace exits 1 and writes nothing; an unknown package name reports E102, and a bare name ambiguous across ecosystems reports E103 listing the qualified candidates.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-INIT-01 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-01', 'criterion': "Before any question, `callisto init` prints `Detected:` with the ecosystems, the origin remote URL, and each package's qualified id, version, last tag, and executable names for executable-producing packages. Artifact selection includes only supported Cargo binary targets."}
CLI-INIT-02 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-02', 'criterion': 'Interactive init asks only: versioning (independent or fixed); whether to ship Cargo binaries as GitHub release assets, only when a supported Cargo binary target exists; the product package, only when more than one Cargo package produces a binary; the forge repository, pre-filled from origin; comma-separated target triples; whether to generate a workflow (default no); and whether to write callisto.toml (default yes).'}
CLI-INIT-03 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-03', 'criterion': 'A supplied flag (--versioning, --artifact-target, --product-package, --forge-repository, --workflow, --no-workflow) skips its question, and declining the final write question writes no file.'}
CLI-INIT-04 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-04', 'criterion': 'The written callisto.toml starts with `# callisto configuration` and contains only answered intent: a `[[fixed-group]]` named `all` listing every non-platform package for fixed versioning, a `[[package]]` `tag-template` for each package whose existing tags follow a non-default convention, and, when shipping binaries, `publish-to` with `github-release` on the selected Cargo product plus a `[release]` table with `product-package`, `forge-repository` and one `[[release.artifact]]` per target and Cargo binary.'}
CLI-INIT-05 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-05', 'criterion': "Tag detection matches each package's tags against its `{display id}@{version}` default, `{name}-v{version}`, `{name}-{version}` and `v{version}`, and writes the single matching non-default convention as that package's tag-template."}
CLI-INIT-06 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-06', 'criterion': 'init exits 1 without writing when a package matches more than one tag convention (listing each with its tags) or when more than one package matches `v{version}`.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-INIT-07 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-07', 'criterion': 'After the config, init prints the first-release preview that `callisto release --dry-run` would show under it, or `No commits yet: commit, then run \\`callisto release --dry-run\\` to preview the first release.` when HEAD has no commit.'}
CLI-INIT-08 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-08', 'criterion': 'A successful init writes callisto.toml and creates .changeset/README.md only when it does not already exist.'}
CLI-INIT-09 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-09', 'criterion': 'init exits 1 without writing when callisto.toml already exists or when the directory is not inside a Git repository, each with a help line naming the fix. With no `origin` remote configured, init instead writes callisto.toml, skips the workflow and shipping-binaries questions, and emits an `init-origin-missing` warning diagnostic; `--workflow` or `--artifact-target` without `origin` exits 1 naming the fix, and `callisto release` refuses later.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-INIT-10 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-10', 'criterion': 'With a non-terminal stdin and no --yes, init exits 1 with `E240` naming --yes and every other missing flag; with --yes it exits 1 with `E241` naming each missing flag: --versioning always, --forge-repository when --artifact-target is given, and --product-package when --artifact-target is given and more than one supported Cargo package produces a binary.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-INIT-11 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-11', 'criterion': 'With --yes, omitting --artifact-target writes no `[release]` table; --artifact-target is repeatable and comma-separated, and --workflow with --no-workflow exits 1 with `E242` before writing.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-INIT-12 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-12', 'criterion': "init exits 1 without writing for an empty or repeated target triple, --artifact-target when no supported Cargo package produces a binary, a --product-package that is absent or not a supported Cargo binary producer, a --forge-repository that is not `owner/repo` or differs from origin's GitHub repository, or --forge-repository or --product-package given without --artifact-target. An npm `bin` or Python `[project.scripts]` product selected for an artifact fails with a typed unsupported-builder diagnostic; registry-only npm and PyPI releases remain valid.", 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-INIT-13 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-13', 'criterion': '`init --format json` writes the human-readable detection, config and preview to stderr and a report `{schemaVersion, command: "init", dryRun, initialized, configPath, config, files}` (plus `diagnostics` when any) to stdout; with --dry-run `dryRun` is true, `initialized` is false, `files` lists every file that would be written, and no file is written.'}
CLI-INIT-14 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-14', 'criterion': "When `.changeset/config.json` exists and declares `fixed`, `linked`, `ignore`, `access`, `baseBranch`, or `updateInternalDependencies` -- none of which callisto translates -- init emits a `changesets-config-key-dropped` warning diagnostic per such key present, naming `[[fixed-group]]` as `fixed`'s callisto.toml equivalent and stating no equivalent exists for the others."}
CLI-INIT-15 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-15', 'criterion': 'When init fails to write callisto.toml, .changeset/README.md, or a generated workflow file, it exits 1 with E278 naming the exact path it failed to write.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-INIT-16 | {'id': 'CLI-INIT-16', 'criterion': 'Cargo binary detection includes every explicit `[[bin]]` name (or path-derived name when omitted), `src/main.rs`, `src/bin/<name>.rs`, and `src/bin/<name>/main.rs`, de-duplicates names, and honors `package.autobins = false` for automatic targets. npm `bin` and Python `[project.scripts]` are detected as executables but are rejected as GitHub artifact builders before init writes files.'}
CLI-WF-01 | {'id': 'CLI-WF-01', 'criterion': 'A workflow is written to .github/workflows/callisto-release.yml only when --workflow is given or the interactive workflow question is answered yes; after writing, init prints that merging to the default branch publishes and advises requiring pull requests and reviews on it.'}
CLI-WF-02 | {'id': 'CLI-WF-02', 'criterion': 'init never overwrites an existing .github/workflows/callisto-release.yml: when a workflow is requested and the file exists, init exits 1 before writing any file.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-WF-03 | {'id': 'CLI-WF-03', 'criterion': "Without artifact slots or platform packages, the generated workflow triggers on push to the detected default branch (local `origin/HEAD`, else a live query of `origin`, else the checked-out branch, else `main`) and has two jobs: `version-pr` running callisto-action with `mode: version-pr` and that same `branch`, and `release`, which runs one at a time per repository (`concurrency` group `callisto-release`, never cancelling a running release), running it with `mode: release` and a token secret per detected ecosystem (CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN, NPM_TOKEN with NPM_CONFIG_PROVENANCE, PYPI_TOKEN) plus GH_TOKEN. These secrets are scoped to the release action step; that action configures npm and Twine native auth only for publishing, supports Bun's token environment, and withholds registry credentials from the Python build backend. Native package-manager publish hooks retain their normal package-owner-defined behavior. When a Python ecosystem is detected, the `release` job installs pinned `build` and `twine` into a venv on the PATH before the release step, with no secrets. init's human-readable output names which of those sources resolved the branch."}
CLI-WF-04 | {'id': 'CLI-WF-04', 'criterion': 'When binaries ship as artifact slots or the workspace has napi platform packages, the generated workflow has `version-pr`, `plan` (runs `callisto release plan --from-release-commit` when the pushed commit adds or modifies .callisto/release-decision.json), `build` (a matrix from napi targets and artifact slots), `build-python` (only when the plan publishes a Python package; builds its sdist and wheel with no credentials) and `execute` (installs a pinned `twine` into a venv on the PATH when the plan publishes a Python package, runs `callisto release artifact-manifest`, sealing those files, and `callisto release execute`) jobs. Registry secrets are scoped to the execute publishing step, never plan or build; npm and Twine receive native auth configuration only during execution, and registry credentials are withheld from Python build subprocesses. Native package-manager publish hooks retain their normal package-owner-defined behavior. The build matrix and napi artifact placement cover only the packages in the release intent, so an unrelated napi addon adds no build and cannot fail the plan.'}
CLI-WF-05 | {'id': 'CLI-WF-05', 'criterion': 'Every callisto-action reference in a generated workflow is pinned to the commit SHA of the `callisto@<cli version>` tag, and init exits 1 when that tag cannot be resolved.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-WF-06 | {'id': 'CLI-WF-06', 'criterion': 'For a workspace with maturin platform targets, or platform packages without napi targets, init prints `Skipping GitHub Actions workflow generation: <reason>.`, records a warning diagnostic in the JSON report, and exits 1 instead when --workflow was given.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-WF-07 | {'id': 'CLI-WF-07', 'criterion': 'callisto-action accepts `mode: version-pr` (default; creates or updates the release PR and outputs `hasChangesets`) and `mode: release` (runs `callisto --format json release` and outputs `published` and `publishedPackages`); any other mode fails the step.', 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-PRE-01 | {'id': 'CLI-PRE-01', 'criterion': "`callisto pre enter <tag>` writes .changeset/pre.json with mode `pre`, the tag and each package's initial version; `callisto pre exit` sets its mode to `exit`. When pre.json already exists with mode `exit`, `pre enter` resumes the cycle: it keeps the existing `initialVersions` and `changesets` and only updates `mode` to `pre` and `tag` to the new value."}
CLI-PRE-02 | {'id': 'CLI-PRE-02', 'criterion': "`pre enter` while pre.json's mode is `pre` exits 1 naming the next step; `pre exit` after prerelease mode was already exited exits 1 naming the next step; `pre exit` with no .changeset/pre.json exits 1 naming `callisto pre enter <tag>`.", 'is_error_case': True}
CLI-SNAP-01 | {'id': 'CLI-SNAP-01', 'criterion': "`callisto snapshot --tag <tag>` sets each SemVer package's manifest version to `0.0.0-<tag>-<short HEAD sha>` and each PEP 440 package's to `0.0.0.dev0+<tag>.<short HEAD sha>`, prints each `from → to`, and leaves changesets in place."}
CLI-COMP-01 | {'id': 'CLI-COMP-01', 'criterion': '`callisto completions <shell>` prints a completion script for bash, elvish, fish, powershell or zsh and exits 0.'}
CLI-SCHEMA-01 | {'id': 'CLI-SCHEMA-01', 'criterion': "`callisto schema` prints the StatusReport JSON Schema; `--type` is a fixed value enum selecting another report type, and an unrecognized value is a clap usage error (exit 2) listing the supported values. Schemas describe the selected payload's serialized values, including string versions, package identities, commit SHAs, repository slugs and digests, not their internal Rust representation. Report payload schemas are distinct from command/dryRun stdout metadata; the release-receipt schema describes the durable receipt file. Native value validity remains enforced by domain readers."}
